  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Increasing winds today, especially across northwest Montana and\n  the higher terrain. \n\n- Scattered rain showers tonight into early Wednesday then slight\n  warming trend into the remainder of the week.\n\n- High confidence in warmer and drier conditions this weekend\n  through much of next week (and maybe beyond).\n  \n\nClouds will increase across northwest Montana today as a \nweakening moisture plume settles along the Canadian border, \ngenerating light orographic showers focused primarily over higher \nterrain. Across the rest of the Northern Rockies, expect partly \ncloudy skies and mild daytime temperatures. Westerly winds will \nstrengthen late this morning into the afternoon, with valley gusts\nof 20-30 mph and persistent, stronger gusts over high terrain \nlasting into early Wednesday. On Flathead Lake, morning southerly \nwinds of 15-20 mph will shift southwesterly in the afternoon, \nwatch for choppy lake conditions.\n\nLight shower activity will gradually drift southward, reaching \nthe Interstate 90 corridor by Wednesday morning. Precipitation \nwill remain predominantly terrain-forced, though isolated light \nshowers may spill into adjacent valleys. Valley rainfall totals \nwill remain minimal (a few hundredths of an inch at most), while \nmountain locations across northwest Montana can expect 0.10 to \n0.30 inches.\n\nBreezy conditions will return Wednesday afternoon while remaining\nshowers diminish. A drier zonal flow pattern will take hold late \nthis week, supporting mostly dry weather and temperatures slightly\nabove seasonal normals. \n\nHigh confidence (80-90% ensemble cluster support) exists for \nstrong upper-level ridging to build over the Northern Rockies this\nweekend and persist into next week. While 850mb temperatures \nsupport daytime highs 10-20 degrees above seasonal averages, \nvalley temperatures may be slightly tempered by autumn valley \ninversions and limited boundary-layer mixing. Conversely, \novernight valley lows are expected to trend cooler than blended \nguidance such as the NBM suggests, as dry air, light winds, and \nclear skies facilitate efficient nocturnal radiational cooling. \nIt's interesting to note that many ensembles point toward warmer \nand drier than normal conditions continuing through the entire \nfirst half of October.",
